## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic. Every rate is integrated
against the delta time the game is given, and every frame is divided into whole
sub-steps of at most `SUBSTEP_MAX` as `specs/simulation.md` states, so an interval of
game time reaches the same state however it was divided into frames. Two more
properties make the game driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
  time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the renderer.
  What a shot destroys, what a discharge takes, and where a dive goes are all decided
  from the field alone.
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses runs off a generator seeded from
  the state's generator field, and it keeps its whole generator state in that field,
  so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly. The
  wave's layout, the choice of which drone dives next, the gap before the next dive,
  a Flux's starting phase, and each burst's scatter are all drawn from it.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and the
surface carries no operation for either. That layer reports which keys are down and
draws and toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The surface
is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through `setAutoStep` and
`advance` below.

Muting is the other. There is no operation that sets it: `mute` is reached the way a
player reaches it, through its binding in `specs/controls.md`, and the snapshot
reports the result.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the audio bus, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root
of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine
advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it drives the actions
the game registers, it owns muting, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.

There is therefore no operation that sets muting: `mute` is reached the way a player
reaches it, through its binding in `specs/controls.md`, and the snapshot reports the
result.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Spectra's, under Diagnostics below.

## Identity

Every drone, every bullet, and every drone-burst carries an `id`: a number, distinct
among the entities live at any moment, reported by `snapshot` and taken by every
per-entity operation. Two rules make an id findable without an assignment scheme:

- An entity added through this surface is appended to its roster, so it is the last
  entry and its id is read from there.
- An id is never reused while the entity holding it is alive, and an entity keeps its
  id for its whole life, across every frame and every phase change. A Prism that
  loses its shell keeps its id.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`SPECTRA_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and the
operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans, and every `x` and
`y` the surface takes or reports for an entity is that entity's center in the stage's
logical units.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation takes the current state, `DeepReadonly<SpectraState>`, as its first
parameter. A pose returns the next `SpectraState`, built from the one it was handed,
which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The signatures below name
the parameters that follow the state, so `setShipBand(band)` below is
`setShipBand(state, band)` on the surface and `snapshot()` is `snapshot(state)`.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}

Each operation takes only the parameters its signature names and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it read
and changes nothing.
{{/if}}

### The core

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `reset(options)` | Restores every declared field of the state to its title-screen value, listed below. |
| `snapshot()` | A pure read of the state, returned as the object under Snapshot shape below. It changes nothing. |
| `menuItemRect(index)` | A pure read of the hit region of item `index` on the menu the current screen shows. It changes nothing. |

`reset` restores `screen` to `"title"`, `phase` to `"live"`, `phaseTimer` to `0`,
`menuIndex` to `0`, `score` to `0`, `lives` to `START_LIVES` (`3`), `stage` to `1`,
`resonance` to `0`, and `inversion` to `0`; it empties the drone, bullet, and burst
rosters and the live discharge; it places the ship at the center of its lane (`640`)
on the cyan band with `0` seconds of fire lockout and `0` seconds of fire cooldown;
it turns the three world gates `waveEntry`, `diveLaunching`, and `ship.contact` back
on; it returns the wave's entry, sway, and dive clocks, and the gap the next dive
waits for, to their fresh-wave values, `diveClock` at `0` and the gap at
`DIVE_FIRST_DELAY`; it sets `extraLifeAwarded` to `false` and `challengeHits` to `0`;
and it sets `simTime` to `0` and the counter the next entity's id is taken from back
to the first id, so two runs reset with the same seed report the same ids for the same
scenario.

`options.seed`, a number defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`), seeds all of the game's
randomness. `muted` is left exactly as it stands, because muting is a player
preference the runtime owns.

`menuItemRect` returns `{ x, y, w, h }` in logical units, with `x` and `y` the
region's top-left corner and `w` and `h` its size: the region a pointer or a touch
contact selects that item from, as `specs/ui.md` states. It returns `null` on the four
screens that show no menu, `howto`, `stageIntro`, `inWave`, and `stageCleared`, and
when `index` names no item of the menu the current screen shows.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### The clock

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setAutoStep(enabled)` | Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. |
| `advance(seconds, frames)` | Runs whole frames of game time immediately. |

`setAutoStep(false)` stops the frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall
clock, so the game changes only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns
it to running itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played. Drawing is
unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows the state the
most recent frame left. It changes no game state.

`advance(seconds, frames)` runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game
time, each worth `seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to
`1`. Each is a real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the
game's own rules produce the result and the canvas reflects it.

Every rate in this game is per second and integrated against the frame's delta time,
and a frame divides into whole sub-steps of at most `SUBSTEP_MAX`, so `advance(1, 1)`
and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same second of game time and reach the same state.

{{/if}}
### The screen and the run

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setScreen(screen)` | Sets the current screen: `"title"`, `"howto"`, `"stageIntro"`, `"inWave"`, `"paused"`, `"stageCleared"`, or `"gameOver"`. |
| `setPhase(phase)` | Sets the sub-phase of the `inWave` screen: `"live"` or `"ready"`. |
| `setPhaseTimer(seconds)` | Sets the seconds left in whichever hold the current screen or phase is running. |
| `setMenuIndex(n)` | Sets the highlighted item of whatever menu the current screen shows, counted from `0`. |
| `setScore(n)` | Sets the score. |
| `setLives(n)` | Sets the lives remaining. |
| `setStage(n)` | Sets the current stage, a whole number from `1`. |
| `setExtraLifeAwarded(awarded)` | Sets whether the run's single extra life has already been awarded. Reported as `extraLifeAwarded`. |
| `setChallengeHits(n)` | Sets how many of the current challenge stage's drones the run has destroyed, a whole number from `0`. It destroys nothing and pays nothing: it is the latch alone, and the bonus belongs to the scoring path. Reported as `challengeHits`. |

`setScore` grants no extra life, whatever boundary it carries the score across: the
award belongs to the scoring path, and this is a precondition. It also leaves
`extraLifeAwarded` exactly as it stands, so posing the score down and back up does
not re-arm the award.

`setStage` spawns nothing and clears nothing. The stage's four derived figures,
`droneSpeedScale`, `bulletSpeedScale`, `diveGapScale`, and `fluxHold`, and
`isChallenge` all follow it, since each is derived from the stage.

### The world gates and the dive clock

Three faculties belong to the wave itself rather than to any one entity. Each has its
own gate, each gates one faculty and nothing else, each is on by default and restored
to on by `reset`, and each is reported by `snapshot`.

| Operation | Gates |
| --- | --- |
| `setWaveEntry(enabled)` | The wave's own release of drones: the staggered groups a stage sends in on the `ENTER_GROUP_GAP` schedule. Off, no drone arrives unless one is added. A drone already traveling its entrance flies it as usual. |
| `setDiveLaunching(enabled)` | The assault's own choice of which formation drone dives next, and when. Off, no dive is launched. A drone already diving flies its dive as usual. |
| `setShipContact(enabled)` | The ship's contact test, which is whether an enemy bullet or a drone body reaching the ship costs a life. Off, the ship still draws, still moves under input, and still fires, and no contact costs anything. It is reported as `ship.contact`. |

The wave carries one clock of its own beside those gates.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setDiveClock(seconds)` | Sets the seconds the wave's dive timer has accumulated since its last launch, `0` for none. It launches nothing itself. `specs/swarm.md` states the rule it feeds. The clock advances only while `diveLaunching` is on. It is reported as `diveClock`. |

### The ship and its cannon

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setShipX(x)` | Places the ship's center at `x` along its lane. The lane's clamp applies, so the ship never lands outside `[SHIP_X_MIN, SHIP_X_MAX]`. |
| `setShipBand(band)` | Sets the ship's band to `"cyan"` or `"magenta"`. It starts no fire lockout: it is the band the ship holds, and `setFireLockout` is what poses the lockout. |
| `setFireLockout(seconds)` | Sets the seconds of post-flip fire lockout remaining, `0` for none. |
| `setFireCooldown(seconds)` | Sets the seconds until the ship may fire again, `0` for none. |
| `setShipContact(enabled)` | The ship's contact gate, stated under The world gates above and repeated here because it is a ship operation. |

There is no operation that flips and no operation that fires. A caller checking the
flip drives the flip action and reads the band and the lockout back; a caller
checking firing drives the fire action and reads the bullets that appear; a caller
that simply wants a bullet in flight places one with `addPlayerBullet`.

### Resonance and the inversion

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setResonance(value)` | Sets the resonance meter, a number from `0` to `RESONANCE_MAX` (`100`). `dischargeReady` follows it. |
| `setInversion(seconds)` | Sets the seconds of spectral inversion remaining, `0` for none. `inversionActive` follows it. |

There is no operation that discharges. A caller checking the discharge poses the
meter and drives the discharge action; the wave that follows is the outcome, so
`discharge.active` and `discharge.radius` are reported and never set.

### The drones

A drone is built one field at a time, so no operation takes a layout.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addDrone(kind, x, y)` | Adds one drone of `kind` (`"shard"`, `"flux"`, or `"prism"`) with its center at a logical stage position. It is appended to the drone roster and takes a fresh id. Its band is `"cyan"`, its phase `"formation"`, its slot the position it was placed at, its band clock `0`, its shell intact,{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}} its charge `0`,{{/if}} and all three of its faculties on. |
| `setDronePosition(id, x, y)` | Places the drone's center at a logical stage position. |
| `setDroneBand(id, band)` | Sets the drone's stored band, on every kind. For a Prism it is the shell's band, and the core's is always the opposite. For a Flux it is the band the drone is holding, or, mid-shimmer, the one it is leaving. It moves the band and nothing else: `bandClock` stays exactly where it stands. |
| `setDronePhase(id, phase)` | Sets the phase: `"entering"`, `"formation"`, `"diving"`, or `"returning"`. |
| `setDroneSlot(id, x, y)` | Sets the center of the drone's resting formation slot. |
| `setDroneBandClock(id, seconds)` | Sets how far a Flux is into its current band window, from `0` to `fluxWindow(stage)`. It moves the clock and nothing else: `shimmer` follows it, and `band` does not change. A Shard and a Prism have no band window to be in, so on either of those `bandClock` stays `0` whatever this is called with. |
| `setDroneShell(id, intact)` | Sets whether a Prism's outer shell stands. A Shard and a Flux have no shell, so on either of those `shellAlive` stays `true` whatever this is called with. |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}
| `setDroneCharge(id, charge)` | Sets the drone's charge, a whole number from `0` to `OVERLOAD_AT` (`3`). |
{{/if}}
| `setDroneTravel(id, enabled)` | Gates the drone's locomotion alone: its advance along an entrance path, its ride on the formation sway, its dive, and its return. Off, it holds its exact center and keeps its phase; nothing is cancelled, completed, or resolved early. Its band clock and its firing run on. |
| `setDroneOscillation(id, enabled)` | Gates a Flux's band clock alone. Off, the Flux holds whichever band or shimmer it is in indefinitely. Its travel and its firing run on. Every drone reports and accepts this gate; on a Shard or a Prism it gates nothing. |
| `setDroneFire(id, enabled)` | Gates the drone's firing alone: the shots it takes during a dive. Off, it flies its whole dive silent. Its travel and its band clock run on. |
| `removeDrone(id)` | Removes the drone with that id. |
| `clearDrones()` | Removes every drone, leaving the bullets and bursts standing. |

A drone's `band` and its `bandClock` are two independent fields, and each has exactly
one setter. `setDroneBand` is the only thing that writes the stored band, and
`setDroneBandClock` is the only thing that writes the position inside the current
band window. The oscillation runs the clock forward, and when it reaches
`fluxWindow(stage)` the Flux flips `band` and returns the clock to `0`, as
`specs/drones.md` states.

### The bullets

One roster holds both kinds, told apart by `friendly`. Ids are unique across the
whole roster.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addPlayerBullet(x, y, band)` | Adds one of the player's bullets in flight, its center at a logical stage position, traveling straight up at `PLAYER_BULLET_SPEED`, carrying `band`. It is appended to the roster with a fresh id and `friendly` true. |
| `addEnemyBullet(x, y, band)` | Adds one enemy bullet in flight, its center at a logical stage position, carrying `band` and traveling straight down at `ENEMY_BULLET_SPEED` multiplied by `bulletSpeedScale(stage)`, the same figure `snapshot().bulletSpeedScale` reports. It is appended with a fresh id and `friendly` false. |
| `setBulletVelocity(id, vx, vy)` | Sets the bullet's velocity, in logical units per second. |
| `removeBullet(id)` | Removes the bullet with that id. |
| `clearPlayerBullets()` | Removes every one of the player's bullets, leaving the enemy bullets, the drones, and the bursts standing. |
| `clearEnemyBullets()` | Removes every enemy bullet, leaving the player's bullets, the drones, and the bursts standing. |

A bullet's band is fixed for its life. Nothing sets it after the fact; a caller that
wants the other band places another bullet.

A bullet `addEnemyBullet` places takes that figure on a challenge stage too, whatever
speed the flyover is running its own fire at.

### The bursts

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `removeBurst(id)` | Removes the live drone-burst with that id. |
| `clearBursts()` | Removes every live drone-burst, leaving the drones and bullets standing. |

There is no operation that adds one. A burst is an outcome of a drone being
destroyed, and the two removals exist so a scenario that has already popped something
can start its count from an empty roster.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns exactly this object. Every field an operation can set is present,
so every operation is verifiable by setting a value and reading it back.

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen:
    "title" | "howto" | "stageIntro" | "inWave" | "paused" | "stageCleared" | "gameOver",
  phase: "live" | "ready",           // the sub-phase of "inWave"
  phaseTimer: <number>,              // seconds left in the current hold
  menuIndex: <number>,               // highlighted item, from 0
  mode: {{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}"overload"{{else}}"sortie"{{/if}},
  stage: <number>,                   // 1 and up
  isChallenge: <boolean>,            // derived: stage % CHALLENGE_EVERY === 0
  score: <number>,
  lives: <number>,
  extraLifeAwarded: <boolean>,       // the run's one extra life has been paid
  challengeHits: <number>,           // challenge drones this stage has destroyed
  resonance: <number>,               // 0..RESONANCE_MAX
  dischargeReady: <boolean>,         // derived: resonance >= RESONANCE_MAX
  inversion: <number>,               // seconds of spectral inversion left, 0 for none
  inversionActive: <boolean>,        // derived: inversion > 0
  muted: <boolean>,
  waveEntry: <boolean>,              // the wave's own release of drones runs
  diveLaunching: <boolean>,          // the assault's own dive choice runs
  diveClock: <number>,               // seconds since the wave's last dive launch
  droneSpeedScale: <number>,         // derived from stage
  bulletSpeedScale: <number>,        // derived from stage
  diveGapScale: <number>,            // derived from stage
  fluxHold: <number>,                // derived from stage, in seconds
  ship: {
    x: <number>,                     // the ship's CENTER
    band: "cyan" | "magenta",
    alive: <boolean>,                // derived: false exactly while phase is "ready"
    lockout: <number>,               // seconds of post-flip fire lockout left
    cooldown: <number>,              // seconds until the next shot is allowed
    contact: <boolean>,              // the ship's contact test runs
  },
  discharge: {
    active: <boolean>,
    radius: <number>,                // the live wave's radius, in logical units
  },
  // Every drone on the field, in roster order.
  drones: [
    {
      id: <number>,
      kind: "shard" | "flux" | "prism",
      x: <number>, y: <number>,      // the drone's CENTER
      band: "cyan" | "magenta",      // its stored band; a Prism's is its SHELL's
      effectiveBand: "cyan" | "magenta",
      phase: "entering" | "formation" | "diving" | "returning",
      slotX: <number>, slotY: <number>,
      bandClock: <number>,           // seconds into the CURRENT window; 0 on other kinds
      shimmer: <boolean>,            // a Flux settled on neither band; false otherwise
      shellAlive: <boolean>,         // a Prism's outer shell stands; true on other kinds
      travel: <boolean>,
      oscillation: <boolean>,
      fire: <boolean>,
{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}
      charge: <number>,              // 0..OVERLOAD_AT
{{/if}}
    },
  ],
  // Every bullet in flight, in roster order.
  bullets: [
    {
      id: <number>,
      x: <number>, y: <number>,      // the bullet's CENTER
      vx: <number>, vy: <number>,    // logical units per second
      band: "cyan" | "magenta",
      effectiveBand: "cyan" | "magenta",
      friendly: <boolean>,           // true = the player's, traveling up
    },
  ],
  // Every drone-burst playing, in roster order.
  bursts: [
    {
      id: <number>,
      x: <number>, y: <number>,      // the burst's CENTER
      size: <number>,                // the footprint the effect is played at
      elapsed: <number>,             // seconds into the effect
      particles: <number>,           // live particles the simulation holds
    },
  ],
  simTime: <number>,                 // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot reports is
what the game holds. The entries built at the call rather than read off a field of
their own, and the notes that go with them:

- `effectiveBand` is the band an entity currently reads and counts as, by the
  definition in `specs/bands.md`: the entity's stored `band`, taken as the opposite
  once for each of the shell being broken, a Flux shimmering, and an inversion being
  active. A player bullet's `effectiveBand` therefore always equals its `band`, and a
  stored-cyan Prism with its shell broken, under an inversion, reads cyan.
- `shimmer` is derived: true exactly while `bandClock >= fluxHold(stage)`, and false
  on a Shard or a Prism. A Flux mid-shimmer is settled on neither band, and its
  `effectiveBand` reports the band it is moving toward, so a reader takes the pair
  rather than the band alone.
- `isChallenge`, `dischargeReady`, `inversionActive`, `ship.alive`,
  `droneSpeedScale`, `bulletSpeedScale`, `diveGapScale`, and `fluxHold` are derived
  at the call from `stage`, `resonance`, `inversion`, and `phase` by the formulas in
  `specs/stages.md`, `specs/resonance.md`, and `specs/bands.md`. Each follows the
  operation that sets what it is derived from.
- The four stage-scaled figures are the formulas read at the stage the snapshot was
  taken at, and nothing else. A challenge stage runs at the stage-1 figures whatever
  stage it falls on, as `specs/stages.md` states, so on a challenge stage what these
  four report is the stage's formula rather than the figure the flyover is running
  at.
- `mode` names the mode this build ships, which `specs/mode.md` defines.
- `muted` is the game's copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every update. No
  operation sets it; the `mute` action does.
- `diveClock` is the wave's own dive timer, `extraLifeAwarded` is the run's
  one-extra-life latch, and `challengeHits` is the current challenge stage's tally of
  drones destroyed. All three are plain settable state rather than derived figures,
  and all three are restored by `reset`.
- `bursts[].particles` is the count the burst's own particle simulation holds at the
  call.
- `simTime` accumulates the time the game's sub-steps cover, as
  `specs/simulation.md` states.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay is read-only and shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources. Register at least:

- the current `screen`, `phase`, and `stage`, and whether the stage is a challenge
  stage;
- the score, the lives, the resonance, and whether a discharge is ready;
- whether a spectral inversion is active and how long is left;
- the ship's `x`, its band, and its remaining fire lockout;
- for each drone, its id, its kind, its stored and effective bands, its position, its
  phase, a Flux's shimmer state, and a Prism's shell state;
{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}
- each drone's charge;
{{/if}}
- how many bullets are in flight, and how many bursts are playing.

Keep each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so
watching the overlay leaves the game exactly as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered sources,
it is shown and hidden by the backtick key as `specs/controls.md` states, it is off
when the game starts, and it reads the game without changing it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Spectra's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read, as
`DeepReadonly<SpectraState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Spectra's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of no
arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the world
the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the panel and
toggling it are the engine's.
{{/if}}
